Entrainment, a well-documented social phenomenon where individuals subtly mimic the behavior of their conversation partners, is a critical factor in determining conversational success. This study introduces a deep neural network approach to model entrainment within spoken dialogue, specifically aiming to measure the distance between two entrainments. We posit that the proposed method can identify the underlying factors driving entrainment and establish a distance metric that aligns with human assessments. Furthermore, we demonstrate that this distance can be effectively utilized to predict human judgments regarding the quality of dialogue interactions. Finally, we show that incorporating this distance as a reward signal in reinforcement learning enhances the ability of a dialogue system to generate high-quality entrainments. Experimental results indicate that the proposed system achieves performance comparable to that of a rule-based approach. The source code is accessible at \url{https://github.com/robinjia123/EntrainmentDistance}.

Keywords: entrainment, spoken dialogue systems, quality metrics, reinforcement learning.
