Temporal action segmentation (TAS) is a critical task in video analysis that involves identifying the start and end points of actions within a clip. While supervised TAS methods have achieved significant success, they depend heavily on extensive annotations. In contrast, unsupervised TAS methods aim to overcome the need for labels by learning directly from raw data. However, existing unsupervised approaches often fail to fully utilize video data or rely on specific data distributions, resulting in suboptimal performance. To address these limitations, we introduce SSCAP, a novel Self-supervised Co-occurrence Action Parsing framework designed for unsupervised temporal action segmentation. SSCAP employs a straightforward yet effective strategy that requires no prior knowledge of data distributions. It first learns a robust video representation using a contrastive learning objective, then generates action labels by modeling the temporal co-occurrence of action segments. Finally, a self-training mechanism is applied to refine the segmentation results. Experiments on two TAS benchmarks demonstrate that SSCAP outperforms current unsupervised methods and achieves competitive results compared to state-of-the-art supervised techniques. The code is accessible at https://github.com/ChenYi-00/SSCAP. Please cite our paper if you utilize our code.
