Neural Architecture Search (NAS) has demonstrated significant potential for automatically discovering high-performing neural networks. However, a persistent challenge in NAS is interference, where previously discovered high-performing architectures fail to generalize when added to the performance model due to non-linearities. This issue often results in models that overfit the search space and yields suboptimal architectures. To address this, we introduce a novel method that mitigates interference by leveraging the concept of "interference-free subspaces." Our approach involves identifying these subspaces and guiding the search to prioritize architectures within them. We evaluate this method across various scenarios, including transfer learning, TF-LNAS, and ENAS, utilizing ResNet and MobileNet as the backbone architectures. Our results demonstrate that the proposed method effectively reduces interference and consistently outperforms baseline approaches. The code is accessible at https://github.com/ashishkumar12345/IF-Search. Additionally, we have released a new Python package, IF-Search, which implements our method and can be installed via `pip install IF-Search`. IF-Search is also available on PyPI. We hope this method proves valuable to the community. Please cite our work if you find it useful. The citation is available at https://github.com/ashishkumar12345/IF-Search.
