This study employs the Poisson-Boltzmann equation to examine how solvent polarization influences the electric double layer forming near a charged soft surface within an electrolyte solution. The findings indicate that accounting for solvent polarization significantly alters the electrostatic potential distribution and the swelling pressure exerted on the polyelectrolyte layer of the charged soft surface. Specifically, when the product of the dielectric constant and temperature for the polyelectrolyte layer exceeds that of the surrounding solution, solvent polarization enhances surface swelling pressure. Conversely, if this product is lower, the swelling pressure is reduced. Furthermore, the range of the electric double layer is diminished by solvent polarization, with this effect becoming more pronounced as the dielectric constant and temperature of the polyelectrolyte layer decrease relative to the solution. These results align with previous experimental observations. Additionally, the impact of solvent polarization on the electrostatic free energy of the polyelectrolyte layer was analyzed, revealing that solvent polarization decreases this free energy. Finally, it was demonstrated that considering solvent polarization is essential for accurately describing the equilibrium state of a charged soft surface in an electrolyte solution. These conclusions are applicable to various systems involving charged soft surfaces, such as biopolymer brushes, colloidal particles, and micelles. Finally, the implications of these findings for the design of soft materials are discussed. This work was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China (Grant No. 21574050). The corresponding author is Prof. Shougen Zhou. The authors declare no conflict of interest.
Keywords: charged soft surface; electric double layer; solvent polarization.
